1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,520
How does the bank know a request really came from the merchant, exactly as the merchant wrote it?

2
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:09,144
Picture a shop's server asking the bank to create a payment.

3
00:00:09,194 --> 00:00:15,000
The request already carries a token, the pass the merchant's API client received from the bank.

4
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:16,944
But a token only says who you are.

5
00:00:16,994 --> 00:00:19,752
It does not say that you wrote this particular request.

6
00:00:19,802 --> 00:00:21,624
And tokens leak.

7
00:00:21,674 --> 00:00:30,192
One ends up in a log file, and anyone who reads that log can now ask for payments of any amount, with the buyer sent back to any address.

8
00:00:30,242 --> 00:00:32,064
There is a second danger.

9
00:00:32,114 --> 00:00:36,600
Someone who records one honest request can simply send it again tomorrow.

10
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:38,832
So a pass alone is not enough.

11
00:00:38,882 --> 00:00:41,976
Think of an old letter closed with a wax seal.

12
00:00:42,026 --> 00:00:45,504
The seal comes from your own ring, so nobody else can make it.

13
00:00:45,554 --> 00:00:48,192
A broken seal shows the letter was opened.

14
00:00:48,242 --> 00:00:50,136
A date shows when it was written.

15
00:00:50,186 --> 00:00:55,032
And a serial number lets the reader notice the same letter arriving twice.

16
00:00:55,082 --> 00:00:58,728
The merchant's server does the same with every request.

17
00:00:58,778 --> 00:01:03,096
It adds a request signature: a seal over every part that matters.

18
00:01:03,146 --> 00:01:05,880
The seal covers the method, which says create.

19
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:09,144
It covers the full address the request goes to.

20
00:01:09,194 --> 00:01:11,016
It covers the token.

21
00:01:11,066 --> 00:01:15,864
It covers a fingerprint of the body, so the amount and the return address are sealed too.

22
00:01:15,914 --> 00:01:18,576
And it covers the time and a one-time number.

23
00:01:18,626 --> 00:01:20,808
The seal is made with a signing key.

24
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:26,832
That is a secret held by only two parties: the bank and this one API client.

25
00:01:26,882 --> 00:01:29,952
The bank writes it straight into its key safe.

26
00:01:30,002 --> 00:01:32,640
The payments app can read it, but never change it.

27
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:36,336
When a request arrives, ecom checks it in a fixed order.

28
00:01:36,386 --> 00:01:40,992
First, the time must be fresh, inside the bank's chosen window.

29
00:01:41,042 --> 00:01:44,040
Second, the body must match its fingerprint.

30
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:49,584
Third, the API client named in the seal must be the one that owns the token.

31
00:01:49,634 --> 00:01:54,528
Last, ecom makes the seal again with the same key and compares the two.

32
00:01:54,578 --> 00:01:56,832
Only then does it spend the one-time number.

33
00:01:56,882 --> 00:02:00,384
That number is the nonce, and each nonce works once.

34
00:02:00,434 --> 00:02:06,504
A nonce is spent only after the seal checks out, so a forger cannot burn a merchant's numbers in advance.

35
00:02:06,554 --> 00:02:08,376
One more rule follows.

36
00:02:08,426 --> 00:02:14,256
Only the merchant's own switched-on website, with one of its own return addresses, can take a payment.

37
00:02:14,306 --> 00:02:16,176
Now watch it from the merchant's server.

38
00:02:16,226 --> 00:02:21,960
It builds the body: the website, the order, the amount in cents and the return address.

39
00:02:22,010 --> 00:02:24,432
It seals the request and sends it.

40
00:02:24,482 --> 00:02:26,304
The answer comes back at once.

41
00:02:26,354 --> 00:02:31,176
The payment is created, and the answer carries the link to the bank's payment page.

42
00:02:31,226 --> 00:02:36,312
In the merchant portal, the payments list shows one new payment, waiting for the buyer.

43
00:02:36,362 --> 00:02:41,304
Now the server sends the very same sealed request a second time, unchanged.

44
00:02:41,354 --> 00:02:42,960
The bank refuses it.

45
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:46,128
The answer says the one-time number is already used.

46
00:02:46,178 --> 00:02:51,216
Next, someone copies a request, changes the amount, and keeps the old seal.

47
00:02:51,266 --> 00:02:54,840
The bank refuses it, because the body no longer matches.

48
00:02:54,890 --> 00:02:57,912
Last, someone sends a request sealed long ago.

49
00:02:57,962 --> 00:03:01,944
The bank refuses that too, because it is no longer fresh.

50
00:03:01,994 --> 00:03:06,432
After every refusal, the payments list stays exactly the same.

51
00:03:06,482 --> 00:03:08,304
Nothing new is created.

52
00:03:08,354 --> 00:03:10,176
What does this protect?

53
00:03:10,226 --> 00:03:13,848
Without the seal, a leaked token is a blank cheque.

54
00:03:13,898 --> 00:03:22,008
Anyone could create payments in the merchant's name, for amounts the merchant never chose, and send the buyer back to a page the attacker controls.

55
00:03:22,058 --> 00:03:26,160
Without the nonce, one recorded request becomes a copy machine.

56
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:30,360
The seal protects the merchant's name and every number in the request.

57
00:03:30,410 --> 00:03:33,408
The date and the nonce protect against copies.

58
00:03:33,458 --> 00:03:38,832
And the key safe protects the key itself, because no person ever types it into the app.

59
00:03:38,882 --> 00:03:40,704
So back to the letter.

60
00:03:40,754 --> 00:03:43,344
The seal says it is yours and unopened.

61
00:03:43,394 --> 00:03:45,384
The date says it is fresh.

62
00:03:45,434 --> 00:03:48,216
The serial number says it is the only one.

63
00:03:48,266 --> 00:03:51,744
Nobody can forge it, and nobody can post it twice.

64
00:03:51,794 --> 00:03:56,664
Next: what happens when the merchant's server asks for the same payment twice on purpose.
